# Git

A question is not permission. `reset`, `rebase`, `push --force`, `push --force-with-lease`, and `branch -D` run only on an order that names the operation and the target SHA or ref.

When asked about such an operation, answer with the current `HEAD` SHA, the target SHA, what would disappear, and the exact command. Then wait.

- The work of an Issue, on its branch, is committed, pushed, and opened as a PR in the turn it is done and the gates pass (`workflow` step 6). Do not leave it uncommitted to wait for an ask.
- Any other commit or push happens only when the message asks for it. An open PR is not consent.
- If history was rewritten, put the SHA from before the rewrite on the first line of the reply.
- Never skip hooks (`--no-verify`) or signing unless the owner orders it.
- A push that follows a test is chained so a failed test stops the push. On PowerShell 5, `&&` is not available: run the test, then push only when the exit code is 0.
- A withdrawn prototype's branch stays until the owner names it.
- An uncommitted change the agent did not make belongs to the owner. Do not commit it with the agent's work, and tell the owner it is there. To branch around it, set it aside with `git stash push -m <what it is> -- <path>` and `git stash pop` it when the work is committed. Do not `restore` or `checkout` it away.

```text
# BAD
Agent: finishes the edits for #11, runs the gates, and asks whether to commit

# GOOD
Agent: finishes the edits for #11, runs the gates, commits, pushes, and opens the PR

# BAD
Agent: finds the owner's edit to docs/roadmap.md in the tree and commits it with #34

# GOOD
Agent: tells the owner, stashes docs/roadmap.md, commits only its own files, and pops it
```
